sequenceDiagram
  %% Current flow: stage 6 of the command development pipeline. Each Node with the nested-fleet switch keeps the nested-fleet Project's default Instance, an Incus container that holds the inner fleet. After each verified Gateway release, when refresh is enabled, Orbit deploys that default to the release commit, refreshes its fleet and takes the seed snapshot that a task or a new Instance of the Project clones. There is no image publish and no daily run. The per-Node run: docs/cli/instance/container/fleet-refresh.mmd.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: development pipeline stage 6 · per-Node seed refresh
  %% kind: pipeline.6-task-image-seed-refresh
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=each Node tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=default tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>one run per Node
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>switched on
  participant C as Default<br/>with inner fleet
  Note over GW: Advance after fleet current<br/>record each Operation
  %%> Triggers:
  %%> - fleet current, when refresh is enabled; the five-minute release tick redrives a missed continuation
  %%> When:
  %%> - a default with a clone copy in flight refuses with `instance.clone_in_flight`; the same release retries
  %%> - a default with a deployment open refuses by its Instance lock; nothing queues behind it
  GW->>SW: Start one run per Node<br/>instance-deploy-direct
  %%> Why:
  %%> - each Node keeps its own seed; nothing is copied between Nodes
  loop each switched-on Node
    SW->>N: Prepare the container<br/>start · fleet check · seed · fetch
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one play: starts the default, checks its running fleet once, keeps the current seed, fetches the release commit
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a later failure then restores a seed that holds the fleet; a failure here changes nothing and restores nothing
    SW->>C: Deploy the release commit<br/>checkout · deploy steps
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the same steps as every container default deployment
    SW->>C: Finish the container<br/>processes · refresh · seed · check
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one play: installs the Processes, refreshes the inner fleet (migrate, converge, Doctor, and `base` retaken for the containers whose disk changed), takes the seed snapshot with the Processes stopped, and checks the default
    %%> - refuses the seed when the Club key is a file on the default's disk; the key lives outside it
    %%> - a default with an inner volume: also snapshots the volume under the same name, right after a flush of the volume (`sync -f`); the two snapshots are the seed, and older pairs go after both new halves exist
  end
  alt the run passes on a Node
    SW-->>GW: Node refreshed<br/>terminal event per Node
    Note over GW: Record the seed commit<br/>clones copy it
    %%> Result:
    %%> - one slow Node does not hold the others; existing clones keep their fleet
    %%> - proof Nodes publish the exact seed commit and timestamp through instance.seed_copy; the release retains those Operation IDs
    %%> - a new copy of a default with an inner volume copies both halves: the volume to its own `inner-<id>`, then the container; while the run is open such a copy refuses with `instance.default_seed_in_flight`
  else a step fails on a Node
    SW->>N: Restore the newest seed<br/>then start the default
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - a default with an inner volume needs both halves of the seed before it restores anything, then restores the container and the volume
    SW--xGW: Node not refreshed<br/>failed step
    Note over GW: Keep the old seed<br/>record failure and retry
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Operation names the failed step; a terminal failure remains visible and can be fixed forward
  end
